\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 1) What is Tree?
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Ans: 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     A tree is a hierarchical data structure. 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Each node can have zero or more child nodes
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     There is a unique path (acyclic) from the root to every other node in the tree.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 2)Key characteristics of a tree include.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Ans:
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Root: The topmost node in the tree from which all other nodes are derived. It serves as the starting point for traversing the tree.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Edge: An edge represents the link or connection between two nodes. 
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Nodes\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 : Each element in the tree is called a node. Nodes can be connected to other nodes through edges.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Parent and Child Nodes: A node that is connected to another node is referred to as the parent node, and the connected node is called its child node.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Leaf Nodes/External Node: A node which don't have any child
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Branch/Internal Nodes: Internal nodes are nodes that have at least one child node. They are not leaf nodes.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Path: A path in a tree refers to a sequence of nodes connected by edges, starting from a specific node and leading to a destination node.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Depth: The depth of a binary tree\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506  is the number of edges on the longest path from the root node to a leaf node. 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Height: The height of a tree is the maximum depth of any node in the tree.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506         Depth: Root to x (destination node) Node
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506         Height: From x (destination) to root node 
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Subtree: A subtree is a smaller tree within a larger tree. 
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Sibling Nodes: Sibling nodes are nodes that have the same parent
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Cousing Nodes: Cousin nodes in a tree refer to nodes that are at the same level or depth but do not share the sam\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 e parent.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Ancestor : In a tree data structure, an ancestor of a node is any node that lies on the path from the root to that specific node.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Descendant : In a tree data structure, a descendant of a node is any node that is reachable by following a path from that specific node.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Diameter: The diameter of a tree is the longest path between any two nodes in the tree.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     (Number of node including two nodes)
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     Degree: The degree of a node in a tree refers to the number of edges connected to that \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
node. It represents the count of immediate connections a node has with other nodes in the tree.
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 3) What is Binary Tree?
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Ans:
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     A binary tree is a hierarchical data structure in which each node has at most two children, referred to as the left child and the right child. 
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 4) Variant of Binary Tree.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Ans:
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     1) Perfect Binary Tree - All level are filled.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     2) Complete Binary Tree - A complete binary tree is a binary tree in which every level is completely filled, except for the last level, which may be filled from left to right.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     3) Full Binary Tree - Every Node has exactly 0 or 2 child
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 5) Tricks of Binary Tree:
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     1) Numb\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 er of Nodes:
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     For a Perfect binary tree: Number of nodes = 2^(height + 1) - 1 
\par 
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     2) Number of Edge:
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506     For a Perfect binary tree: Number of edges = 2^(height + 1) - 2
